// WHAT THIS FILE DOES
// The security rules for the Firestore database in the chernozem-danny
// Firebase project. They replace the "test mode" rules, which let anyone
// read and write everything and stopped working after 30 days.
//
// The rule: a request is allowed only when it comes from someone signed in
// with Firebase Authentication (Google, or an e-mail and a password).
// Everyone else is refused. It covers every collection, including ones
// added later.
//
// What uses Firestore today:
//   - This app (src/, server/) does not read or write Firestore at all.
//   - scripts/fetch-ndvi.js and scripts/seed-fields.js write "fields" and
//     "fields/<id>/readings". They sign in with the service-account key
//     through firebase-admin, which is not checked by these rules, so they
//     keep working.
//   - "notes" holds the notes from the old Notes page.
//   - William's app shares the project. Any collection it uses is covered
//     by the same rule, so it works for his signed-in users.
//
// Note: anyone can create an account on the Register page, so "signed in"
// means "has an account", not "is on the team".
//
// WHO EDITS IT
// Danny. Deploy a change with:
//   npx firebase-tools deploy --only firestore:rules --project chernozem-danny

rules_version = '2';

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {
    // Every document, in every collection and subcollection.
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if request.auth != null;
    }
  }
}
